{% extends "pages/_guide.jinja" %}
{% set guide_slug = 'fixtures-and-state' %}
{% set thesis = 'There is no World struct to define. Fixtures are the world: each scenario gets fresh ones, steps borrow them, and anything shared across scenarios is shared on purpose.' %}
{% set sections = [
  {'id': 'fixtures', 'label': 'Fixtures are the world'},
  {'id': 'mutable', 'label': 'Changing a fixture'},
  {'id': 'slots', 'label': 'Optional state in slots'},
  {'id': 'shared', 'label': 'Sharing across scenarios'},
  {'id': 'fine-print', 'label': 'Fine print'},
] %}
{% block guide %}
          <section id="fixtures" aria-labelledby="fixtures-title">
            <h2 id="fixtures-title">Fixtures are the world</h2>
            <p>
              A step parameter that is not a placeholder is a fixture, found by
              its name. The fixtures are ordinary <code>rstest</code> fixtures,
              so a unit test and a scenario can share one definition.
            </p>
            {% call ui.code_panel('tests/lantern.rs', sample='tests/lantern.rs') %}
            {% highlight 'rust', 'lantern-syntax' %}
#[fixture]
fn trolley() -> Trolley {
    Trolley::default()
}
            {% endhighlight %}
            {% endcall %}
            <p>
              The scenario function names the fixtures the scenario owns, and
              every scenario builds its own. Two scenarios never see each
              other’s trolley, so they can run in parallel and in any order.
            </p>
          </section>

          <section id="mutable" aria-labelledby="mutable-title">
            <h2 id="mutable-title">Changing a fixture</h2>
            <p>
              A step that takes <code>&amp;mut Trolley</code> gets exclusive
              access to the scenario’s trolley for the length of the step; one
              that takes <code>&amp;Trolley</code> reads it. No
              <code>RefCell</code> is needed for either.
            </p>
            {% call ui.code_panel('tests/lantern.rs', sample='tests/lantern.rs') %}
            {% highlight 'rust', 'lantern-syntax' %}
#[when("the departure bell rings")]
fn depart(trolley: &mut Trolley) {
    trolley.arrived = true;
}

#[then("the lantern arrives upright")]
fn check(trolley: &Trolley) {
    assert!(trolley.loaded);
    assert!(trolley.arrived);
    assert!(trolley.upright);
}
            {% endhighlight %}
            {% endcall %}
            <p>
              A step can also return a new value instead, which replaces the
              fixture of that type; <a href="/rstest-bdd/guides/steps/#returns">steps that return</a>
              shows how.
            </p>
          </section>

          <section id="slots" aria-labelledby="slots-title">
            <h2 id="slots-title">Optional state in slots</h2>
            <p>
              Some state only exists once a step has run: what was borrowed,
              what a command printed. <code>Slot&lt;T&gt;</code> starts empty
              and is filled by <code>set</code>, read by <code>get</code>, and
              checked by <code>is_empty</code>, all through a shared reference.
              A struct of slots derives <code>ScenarioState</code>, which adds
              <code>reset</code> to empty them all.
            </p>
            {{ ui.sample('tests/features/props.feature') }}
            {{ ui.sample('tests/props.rs') }}
            {% call ui.note('Derive Default as well', 'caveat') %}
            <p>
              In rstest-bdd {{ rstest_bdd_version }},
              <code>#[derive(ScenarioState)]</code> requires
              <code>Default</code> but does not provide it, so derive both, as
              above. The user’s guide says the derive supplies
              <code>Default</code>; the published release does not.
            </p>
            {% endcall %}
          </section>

          <section id="shared" aria-labelledby="shared-title">
            <h2 id="shared-title">Sharing across scenarios</h2>
            <p>
              Share infrastructure, not scenario data. The cupboard above is
              built once, by a <code>#[once]</code> fixture, and never changed;
              each scenario builds its own <code>Stage</code> and
              <code>Rehearsal</code> from it. The second scenario states its
              own starting point rather than relying on whatever the first
              left behind.
            </p>
            <p>
              A <code>#[once]</code> fixture is a <code>&amp;'static</code>
              reference, so steps take it through a per-scenario fixture, as
              <code>Stage</code> does here, rather than by name.
            </p>
          </section>

          <section id="fine-print" aria-labelledby="fine-print-title">
            <h2 id="fine-print-title">Fine print</h2>
            {% call ui.note('Mutable shared state is a hazard', 'caveat') %}
            <p>
              A <code>#[once]</code> fixture with interior mutability couples
              every scenario that touches it to the order the test runner
              picks. Keep shared fixtures read-only, or give each scenario its
              own transaction or directory.
            </p>
            {% endcall %}
          </section>
{% endblock %}
